   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the energy law, in relation to directing the state energy planning board to conduct a study of natural gas reliability",
    "basePrintNo": "S6336",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6336-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6-108, Energy L",
    "lawSection": "Energy Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6336\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the energy law, in relation to\ndirecting the state energy planning board to conduct a study of\nnatural gas reliability\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis bill would require the New York State Energy Planning Board to\nundertake a study of the overall reliability of the State's supply of\nnatural gas and to prepare a report and on such study and transmit the\nreport and reliability study to the governor and the legislature.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of the bill provides that before January 1, 2018, the\nState Energy Planning Board shall contract with an independent and\ncompetitively bid contractor with experience in natural gas to study\nthe overall reliability of the State's supply of natural gas. The\nstudy would include an assessment of the need for additional\ntransmission of natural gas in the State and to states in New England;\nthe integrity and impacts of new natural gas infrastructure as it\nrelates to the environment; the consequences to existing natural gas\ncustomers in the State of constraints in the natural gas delivery\nsystem; the economic demand for additional natural gas from\nresidential, commercial, manufacturing and industrial sectors in the\nState; and the status of and future opportunities for the\nimplementation of natural gas expansion under public service\ncommission proceedings and the State Energy Plan.\n\nSection two of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe Clean Energy Standard in New York State is directed at\nsignificantly expanding the role of renewables in New York's energy\nmarket with a goal of 50% renewables by 2030. In implementing the\nexpansion to renewables, the state should not lose focus on the\nimportance of natural gas in generating cleaner electricity, heating\nhomes and powering businesses in New York while providing affordable\nenergy with a positive impact on air emissions. Natural gas is an\nimportant bridge to obtaining the goal of continued carbon reduction.\nThe objective of the study in this bill is to refocus on the\nreliability of natural gas to determine if any modification is\nrequired to the State's Energy Plan. The energy sector is constantly\nevolving and the energy plan for natural gas should be reviewed and\nupdated if necessary.\n\nReliability risks should be assessed. There are a number of commercial\nand industrial customers that are currently unable to access\nuninterruptible natural gas service. There are also residential\ncustomers that want to access natural gas but are unable to connect.\n\nPipeline development and infrastructure issues are impacting not only\nNew York State but also New England. Constraints in the delivery\nsystem particularly in the winter months has significant price\nconsequences. Planning for the natural gas market will help prevent\npolar vortex-type price spikes and place New York in a better position\nto transition its energy market. Natural gas provides a significant\nand real contribution to business and residential customers. This bill\nseeks to ensure that appropriate planning and actions are taken to\nguarantee that there is reliability in the supply of natural gas.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately\n",
