    "lawCode": "Amd §6-108, Energy L",
    "lawSection": "Energy Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6336A\n\nSPONSOR: GRIFFO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the energy law, in relation to directing the state ener-\ngy planning board to conduct a study of natural gas reliability\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would require the New York State Energy Planning Board to\nundertake a study of the overall reliability of the State's supply of\nnatural gas and to prepare a report and on such study and transmit the\nreport and reliability study to the governor and the legislature.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill provides that before January 1, 2019, the State\nEnergy Planning Board shall contract with an independent and competi-\ntively bid contractor with experience in natural gas to study the over-\nall reliability of the State's supply of natural gas. The study would\ninclude an assessment of the need for additional transmission of natural\ngas in the State and to states in New England; the integrity and impacts\nof new natural gas infrastructure as it relates to the environment; the\nconsequences to existing natural gas customers in the State of\nconstraints in the natural gas delivery system; the economic demand for\nadditional natural gas from residential, commercial, manufacturing and\nindustrial sectors in the State; and the status of and future opportu-\nnities for the implementation of natural gas expansion under public\nservice commission proceedings and the State Energy Plan.\n\nSection two of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Clean Energy Standard in New York State is directed at significantly\nexpanding the role of renewables in New York's energy market with a goal\nof 50% renewables by 2030. In implementing the expansion to renewables,\nthe state should not lose focus on the importance of natural gas in\ngenerating cleaner electricity, heating homes and powering businesses in\nNew York while providing affordable energy with a positive impact on air\nemissions. Natural gas is an important bridge to obtaining the goal of\ncontinued carbon reduction.\n\nThe objective of the study in this bill is to refocus on the reliability\nof natural gas to determine if any modification is required to the\nState's Energy Plan. The energy sector is constantly evolving and the\nenergy plan for natural gas should be reviewed and updated if necessary.\n\nReliability risks should be assessed. There are a number of commercial\nand industrial customers that are currently unable to access uninterrup-\ntible natural gas service. There are also residential customers that\nwant to access natural gas but are unable to connect.\n\nPipeline development and infrastructure issues are impacting not only\nNew York State but also New England. Constraints in the delivery system\nparticularly in the winter months has significant price consequences.\nPlanning for the natural gas market will help prevent polar vortex-type\nprice spikes and place New York in a better position to transition its\nenergy market. Natural gas provides a significant and real contribution\nto business and residential customers. This bill seeks to ensure that\nappropriate planning and actions are taken to guarantee that there is\nreliability in the supply of natural gas.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017 - Passed Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately\n",
